    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Adiabatic accessibility",
        "statement": "Fix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. For $x,y\\in D_c$, write $x\\prec_c y$, and say that $y$ is adiabatically accessible from $x$, exactly when the declared process model admits at least one record $P\\in\\mathcal P_s$ from [[def-td-process-and-protocol]] with $x_P^-=x$, $x_P^+=y$, $Q[P]=0$, and the model’s stated auxiliary and work-device endpoint conditions satisfied. The material constitution is the same at both endpoints. The process record must account for the selected outer boundary and every auxiliary included by its protocol.\n\nThe sign convention is $Q>0$ into the system and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}>0$ for work done on it, as fixed in [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. Thus adiabatic means zero protocol-assigned heat, not zero work, zero energy change, isolation, quasistatic evolution, or reversibility. The relation concerns equilibrium endpoints only; intermediate configurations may be nonequilibrium. Existence of a witness record is model input, not a consequence of this definition.\n\nWhen the constitution is clear, write $x\\prec y$. Define $x\\sim y$ by $x\\prec y$ and $y\\prec x$, and $x\\prec\\prec y$ by $x\\prec y$ and not $y\\prec x$. These are respectively accessibility equivalence and strict one-way accessibility; neither is defined by equality of energy alone.\n\nThe order-theoretic relation of Lieb and Yngvason is an abstract model of this operation. Goddard’s Carathéodory formulation uses a distinct local-inaccessibility route; it is cited as context and does not supply the product comparison results used later.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "def-td-process-and-protocol",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-process-and-protocol": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.A–II.B",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §2",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
            },
            {
              "title": "Samuel J. Ling, William Moebs, and Jeff Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §4.1 Reversible and Irreversible Processes",
              "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-1-reversible-and-irreversible-processes"
            },
            {
              "title": "Goddard, The second law of thermodynamics as variation on a theme of Carathéodory, §§2–3",
              "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt27k6m59q/qt27k6m59q.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Model-defined transitions between equilibrium states of a fixed thermodynamic composition when the admitted process has no heat transfer; no claim is made that a proposed transition is physically realizable without a process witness."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Tagged scaled systems and separated products",
        "statement": "Fix a family $I$ of primitive material labels. A tagged type $c\\in I$ retains its identity even if another type has the same coordinate domain. A positive scale $\\lambda>0$ is dimensionless and is represented by the separately tagged bulk copy $c^{(\\lambda)}$ and map $\\delta_\\lambda$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A finite separated compound is a finite tuple of tagged scaled systems, with permutation and reassociation identifications and an empty unit type. Its state is a tuple of constituent equilibrium states; its extensive coordinate tuple is the sum specified by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] whenever the pair is admitted there.\n\nThe formal composition vector of a compound records, for each primitive label $c$, the sum of its positive scale coefficients. Two compounds have the same composition exactly when these vectors agree. The distinct **formal mass** $m(A)$ is the sum of all scale coefficients in $A$, a dimensionless bookkeeping quantity; it is not physical mass in kilograms, mole amount, energy, or volume. Same-composition compounds have equal formal mass. For states $A,B$ of the same compound type, write $A\\prec B$ when the declared adiabatic-accessibility relation of [[def-td-adiabatic-accessibility]] admits that endpoint comparison. This definition does not infer accessibility from coordinate addition.\n\nAn equal-composition comparison is a comparison between states of types with the same formal composition vector. The later reaction-network definition may separately admit transitions between distinct composition types, but the mathematical supplier still requires equal formal mass at both endpoints. A physical application must state how its channel preserves the chosen formal mass and separately record its dimensioned conservation balances. The empty unit has one state and formal mass zero. No identity is imposed between a primitive type and a scaled tag, or between two different decompositions of a compound, beyond the explicitly declared reassociation, permutation, and bulk-composition maps.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity": "physical-assumption"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.A–II.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §2",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "The formal state and comparison bookkeeping for the declared finite family of separated bulk systems; mixed-composition transitions are excluded here and are defined separately."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Accessibility axioms A1–A6",
        "statement": "Adopt the following axioms for $\\prec$ on every declared pair of finite compounds with equal formal mass in [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]]. Initially this covers equal-composition adiabatic accessibility. If a later channel model admits comparisons between distinct composition types, the same axioms apply to those comparisons and all their finite scaled products; no cross-type transition is admitted by this postulate alone. They are assumptions of the operational model, not consequences of the process definition or of energy additivity.\n\n**A1 (reflexivity).** $A\\prec A$ for every state $A$.\n\n**A2 (transitivity).** If $A\\prec B$ and $B\\prec C$, then $A\\prec C$.\n\n**A3 (consistency).** If $A\\prec B$, then $A+C\\prec B+C$ for every finite spectator compound $C$ for which both endpoint compounds are admitted and the resulting formal masses agree.\n\n**A4 (scaling).** If $A\\prec B$, then $\\lambda A\\prec\\lambda B$ for every $\\lambda>0$, using the tagged scaling maps of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]].\n\n**A5 (splitting).** For each state $x$, positive $s<t$, and tagged scaled copies of its material, the state $t x$ is accessible in both directions to the separated pair $(s x,(t-s)x)$. This states reversible splitting of an equilibrium preparation into scaled copies, not additivity of arbitrary state functions.\n\n**A6 (stability).** Let $A,B$ have equal formal mass. Let $Z_0,Z_1$ be fixed positive spectator compounds of equal formal mass, and let $\\epsilon_n>0$ decrease to zero. If $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\prec B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for every $n$, then $A\\prec B$.\n\nEquality of formal masses in A6 is required so each comparison is defined; equality of composition vectors is not required when a declared model includes cross-type transitions. A6 is a limiting closure assumption on the relation; it does not assert continuity of any physical process or the existence of a limiting apparatus.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity": "operational-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §II.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, Appendix A-1–A-6",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
            },
            {
              "title": "Goddard, The second law of thermodynamics as variation on a theme of Carathéodory, §3",
              "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt27k6m59q/qt27k6m59q.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Adopted order assumptions for adiabatic accessibility on equal-formal-mass finite compounds in the declared family, including cross-type comparisons only when a channel network later admits them; they do not establish that an actual material family satisfies them."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-thermal-join",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Thermal joins and thermal equivalence",
        "statement": "For the simple-compressible physical branch, a primitive label is a pair $i=(c_i,n^i)$ of constitution and fixed positive mole amounts. Its numerical state domain is exactly $\\Gamma_i=\\Gamma_{c_i,n^i}$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], with embedding $b_i(u,v)=(u,v,\\hat n^i)\\in D_{c_i}$. The unit map sends $(u,v)$ to $(u\\,\\mathrm J,v\\,\\mathrm{m^3})$; thus $u$ is the numerical value of the fixed internal energy, not a newly oriented energy coordinate. The underlying accessibility is the pullback of the admitted physical endpoint relation along $b_i$. Positive bulk scaling uses the declared scaling of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], carries the mole vector to $\\lambda n^i$, and retains the distinct scaled tag. More general work coordinates or formal thermometer examples require their own explicitly declared domain, coordinate unit map and endpoint relation; no identification with $D_c$ is supplied by this definition.\n\nFor two named simple-system types $i,j$ with energy/work coordinates $x=(u_i,v_i)$ and $y=(u_j,v_j)$, a thermal join is the model-declared compound state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ whose energy coordinate is $u_i+u_j$ and whose work coordinates retain the ordered pair $(v_i,v_j)$. Its join domain consists exactly of those tuples $(u,v_i,v_j)$ for which there are $u_i,u_j$ with $(u_i,v_i)$ and $(u_j,v_j)$ in the component domains and $u=u_i+u_j$. This is a tagged join state, distinct from the separated pair $(x,y)$, even when their total coordinates agree. The joined domain retains both work coordinates and has aggregate mole composition $n^i+n^j$ (in a common declared species list); it is not thereby a simple-compressible slice of one $D_c$. The contact model supplies its joined equilibrium endpoint space, endpoint coordinate map and admitted process relation. The contact model declares the pair encoding and join encoding to have the same aggregate composition and extends its accessibility relation to these two encodings; the contact axioms specify which such comparisons are admitted.\n\nWrite $x\\sim_T y$ exactly when the pair $(x,y)$ and its join state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ are accessible in both directions under the declared contact operation. Here $\\sim_T$ is a relation between states of the two named systems; it is not numerical equality of temperature. Nested joins use only the model’s declared reassociation and permutation identifications. The coordinate sum alone supplies no accessibility comparison.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity": "physical-assumption"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §IV.A",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–3",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "The energy-summing contact operation for the declared simple-system family; it does not prescribe a temperature scale or guarantee that contact equilibrates any chosen pair."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-cross-type-reaction-network",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Cross-type mixing and reaction network",
        "statement": "A cross-type mixing and reaction network consists of a declared set $\\mathcal G$ of tagged composition types and a state set $X_\\Gamma$ for each type. Each state set comes with a fixed formal composition $\\nu_\\Gamma$, and its states satisfy $\\nu(x)=\\nu_\\Gamma$ as in [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]]. These mathematical coefficients and their positive type weights are distinct from dimensioned material amounts. For physical channels the model additionally supplies endpoint quantity maps $U_\\Gamma:X_\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ and $n_\\Gamma:X_\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R^r\\,\\mathrm{mol}$ in a declared finite species list, together with any separately tracked mechanical or external-field endpoint quantities. No identification of two distinct scaled tags is made.\n\nFor each channel $\\alpha$ from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$, the model supplies sets $Z_\\alpha$ of configurations, $\\mathcal K_\\alpha$ of protocol records, $\\mathcal B_\\alpha$ of outer-boundary records, and $\\mathcal R_\\alpha$ of transfer records, and equilibrium subsets and endpoint maps $\\xi^-_\\alpha:Z^-_\\alpha\\to X_\\Gamma$, $\\xi^+_\\alpha:Z^+_\\alpha\\to X_{\\Gamma'}$. A typed record is\n$$P=(I,x^-,x^+,z,\\kappa,b,r),$$\nwhere $I=[t_0,t_1]$, $t_0<t_1$, is a finite time interval in seconds, $x^-\\in X_\\Gamma$, $x^+\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$, $z:I\\to Z_\\alpha$, $z(t_0)\\in Z^-_\\alpha$, $z(t_1)\\in Z^+_\\alpha$, $\\xi^-_\\alpha(z(t_0))=x^-$, $\\xi^+_\\alpha(z(t_1))=x^+$, $\\kappa\\in\\mathcal K_\\alpha$, $b\\in\\mathcal B_\\alpha$, and $r\\in\\mathcal R_\\alpha$. Transfer maps $Q_\\alpha,W_{\\mathrm{on},\\alpha}:\\mathcal R_\\alpha\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ and $N_\\alpha:\\mathcal R_\\alpha\\to\\mathbb R^r\\,\\mathrm{mol}$ record heat, work and material transfer with declared signs; any tracked $\\Delta K_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ and $\\Delta U_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ are also joule-valued record maps. The channel specifies a balance predicate on this typed record set, or equivalently a balance map into a declared product of quantity spaces whose required value is zero, and a model-admitted subset satisfying that predicate. The predicate states the actual energy and material constraints of that channel; no balance law, physical admission or endpoint realizability follows from the tuple alone. For a fixed-constitution core closed channel, its endpoint embeddings, fields and admitted subset are exactly those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]], with no material transfer and its stated kinetic/external restrictions. Other channels require their own adopted constraints. Define $(\\Gamma,x)\\mathcal R(\\Gamma',y)$ exactly when an admitted channel record has those endpoints. No channel is inferred from separated composition.\n\nFor any permitted equal-formal-mass compound states $A,B$ of the network, define $A\\prec B$ exactly when at least one admitted channel record has initial state $A$ and final state $B$; for named single types this is the relation $\\mathcal R$ above. This is the exact relation supplied to [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], not a separate abstract order that enlarges or restricts the channel edges. Its extension to finite tagged products and common spectators is governed by the adopted A1–A6 conditions below. Thus every edge in a decorated chain is precisely an admitted channel transition. This identification is a declared model condition.\n\nThe network is **finite-factor closed** when its types admit the declared finite composition and positive scaling operations and each allowed channel respects the model’s stated conservation constraints. In addition, each channel must preserve the dimensionless formal mass $m$ of [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]]; this is the equal-coefficient-mass convention required by the mathematical chain-cost supplier, not a statement that formal mass is kilograms or moles. The common relation on all equal-formal-mass compounds satisfies A1–A6 of [[post-td-accessibility-a1-a6]], including consistency with spectators and stability. A decorated finite chain is exactly a finite sequence of such allowed transitions with a common catalyst and the real baseline-entropy edge costs in [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]].\n\nFor $\\Gamma,\\Gamma'$, let $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$ be the infimum of those finite decorated-chain costs, with $+\\infty$ if no witness exists and $-\\infty$ if costs are unbounded below. The network has **no sinks** when, for every ordered type pair,\n\n$$F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)<+\\infty.$$\n\nNo-sinks is an explicit property required of a network used in the global-offset theorem; it is not proved by this definition, and it is not asserted for every physical mixing or reaction. The definition permits the channel set to be empty; then the condition must still be checked rather than inferred.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-process-and-protocol",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-process-and-protocol": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6": "physical-assumption",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§VI.A–VI.B",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 15,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "A declared family of allowed transitions between distinct tagged composition types, with finite catalyst chains and an explicit no-sinks condition; this definition does not claim a particular material network satisfies it."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Simple-system geometric axioms",
        "statement": "For each simple-system chart $\\Gamma_i\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_i+1}$, $n_i\\ge1$, let $x=(u,v)$, with one energy coordinate $u$ and $n_i$ work coordinates $v$. The chart is a nonempty open convex domain. The simple-compressible physical case is the fixed-composition slice and embedding of [[def-td-thermal-join]], with $n_i=1$, $u=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $v=V/(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})$. A declared multi-work chart or joined chart instead supplies its own equilibrium endpoint domain and diagonal unit map: $U=u(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $W_a=v_a w_a$ for fixed positive work-coordinate units $w_a$. The numerical domain is identified with these physical endpoints by that supplied map, and accessibility is pulled back from the admitted endpoint relation. These identifications preserve the internal-energy meaning and fixed composition; extra work coordinates do not follow from the simple-compressible state definition. Adopt these assumptions for every state $x$ and for every thermal join treated as a simple system:\n\n**A7 (convex-combination accessibility).** For $0<t<1$, the separated scaled pair $(t x,(1-t)y)$ is accessible to the chart state $t x+(1-t)y$, whenever these states are in the declared domains.\n\nFor each $x$, define its forward sector $A_x=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:x\\prec y\\}$. Adopt:\n\n**S1 (strict successor).** Some $y\\in\\Gamma_i$ satisfies $x\\prec\\prec y$.\n\n**S2 (unique finite-slope support).** At $x=(u_x,v_x)$, $A_x$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane through $x$, and it can be written\n$$\nu-u_x+P_i(x)\\cdot(v-v_x)=0\n$$\nwith finite $P_i(x)\\in\\mathbb R^{n_i}$. The map $P_i:\\Gamma_i\\to\\mathbb R^{n_i}$ is locally Lipschitz: every point has a neighborhood on which one finite Lipschitz constant bounds $\\|P_i(x)-P_i(y)\\|/\\|x-y\\|$. A supporting hyperplane means $A_x$ lies in one of its closed half-spaces and meets it at $x$.\n\n**S3 (connected relative boundary).** The boundary $\\partial_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$, taken in the topology relative to $\\Gamma_i$, is arcwise connected.\n\nThese are explicit adopted conditions. The slope $P_i$ is a geometric support parameter here; no equation identifying it with measured pressure is asserted. In particular, S1–S3 and A7 do not assert comparison hypothesis for products.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-thermal-join",
          "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-thermal-join": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6": "operational-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§III.A–III.B",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §2",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
            },
            {
              "title": "Goddard, The second law of thermodynamics as variation on a theme of Carathéodory, §3",
              "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt27k6m59q/qt27k6m59q.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Adopted convexity, irreversibility, support regularity and boundary-connectivity conditions on the forward sectors of simple thermodynamic state domains and admitted joins."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-thermal-join-axioms",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Thermal-contact axioms T1–T4",
        "statement": "Use thermal joins and $\\sim_T$ exactly as defined in [[def-td-thermal-join]]. For every ordered pair of simple systems and every admitted finite join, adopt:\n\n**T1 (joining).** $(x,y)\\prec J_{ij}(x,y)$.\n\n**T2 (reversible splitting).** Every join state has at least one split into component states at its stated total energy and work coordinates that is reversible to the join state. Also, for every state $x$ and every $0<t<1$, the equal-state scaled split $(t x,(1-t)x)$ is reversible to $x$.\n\n**T3 (thermal-equilibrium transitivity).** If $x\\sim_T y$ and $y\\sim_T z$ in the model’s declared contact family, then $x\\sim_T z$. This requires the model to provide the relevant joined types and identifications.\n\n**T4 (transversality).** For each simple-system state $x$, there are $x_0,x_1$ in that system with $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$ and $x_0\\sim_T x_1$.\n\nThe axioms apply to admitted types and their joins only. T4 is an assumption about the contact relation; it is not a consequence of an equation of state or of a thermometer reading. The stronger range condition T5 is stated separately in [[post-td-universal-thermal-range]], and join coherence C1 is separately stated in [[post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence]].",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-thermal-join",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
          "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-thermal-join": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6": "operational-prerequisite",
          "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms": "operational-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §IV.A",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–3",
