      {
        "id": "pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Entropy representation for finite separated products",
        "statement": "Let $I$ be a nonempty, possibly infinite set of primitive system labels. Assume the declared family includes every finite separated product and every finite thermal join, and that every finite join-closed subfamily satisfies the hypotheses of [[pthm-td-contact-product-comparison]]. Also assume the explicit finite-factor representation of [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]] and the Axiom of Choice. Then there is a single dimensionless function $\\widetilde S$ on all states of the declared finite-factor family that is additive under separation, extensive under every admitted positive scaling, and characterizes every equal-composition comparison:\n\n$$ A\\prec B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\widetilde S(A)\\le \\widetilde S(B). $$\n\nFor each simple chart, A7 also makes $\\widetilde S$ concave on every convex region where the stated recombination operation is admitted. The result does not set the entropy unit to $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, determine additive constants across different reaction compositions, or establish empirical validity of the contact assumptions.",
        "deps": [
